diff --git a/docs/priority-probe-20261031/README.md b/docs/priority-probe-20261031/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..5fc2a68e93226d57c31f33b32ee3524c0bd99d0f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/priority-probe-20261031/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,63 @@
+# Samsung priority entry-probe fallback
+
+Phone observation: SM-S948B, firmware S948BXXU5BZJA, kernel
+6.12.69-android16-6-pa4ef354-abogkiS948BXXU5BZJA-4k. Test3 reported
+`init_errno=-14 stage=2 op=0`, both installation masks zero, compat table present,
+and `native_insn=compat_insn=d503233f`. This localizes the optional layer failure
+to the first table hook's no-fault write, before automatic selection is enabled.
+Repeated rebooting cannot change this write failure. Core SusFS remains active.
+
+The existing syscall-table path remains first choice. Only -EFAULT, after the
+FP helper has restored every published entry and drained callbacks, admits the
+entry-probe route. No protected-write workaround or kernel protection setting is
+added. All four kernel targets must be proven named core entry starts. Native
+and compat table entries must share each target (the four int-only priority
+APIs do so in the previously reviewed BZJA image). Separate compat targets,
+foreign hooks, missing/unreadable tables or entries, CONFIG_ARM64_BTI_KERNEL,
+and explicit BTI c/jc landing instructions disable this optional feature.
+
+The exact inspected addresses are registered. A post handler keeps probes
+unoptimized: Linux jump-optimized kprobes ignore pre-handler PC redirection.
+Each pre handler leaves the frame unchanged when passing through, or sets the
+return value and returns to the incoming LR before the original entry's PAC.
+The shared decision function preserves root/system/trusted/self/namespace,
+PID/process-group and default-UID exclusions and I/O-priority error ordering.
+It allocates nothing, takes no mutex and does no VFS/package work in a probe.
+KernelSU predicates are the existing non-sleeping policy lookups.
+
+Ready is published only after four successful registrations and a second table
+identity check. Partial failures unregister every armed probe. Exit disables
+filtering first, unregisters probes, then drains counters and Tasks RCU return
+tails before releasing policy/helper pointers. Probe addresses and disabled
+flags are reset for reuse. Existing table restoration/draining is unchanged.
+
+Status adds `route` (0=off, 1=table, 2=entry probes), `probe_mask` and
+`table_errno`. `init_errno=0 stage=0 route=2 probe_mask=f table_errno=-14` is the
+expected successful fallback; configuration/automatic flags are independent.
+Stage 4 identifies a refused or failed fallback. No function addresses are
+printed through status. All status fields retain the existing root-only mode.
+
+Recorded host evidence: all 29 suites / 56 normal and ASan/UBSan runner
+executions passed. Actual production C is exercised with CONFIG_COMPAT on/off,
+all four table/registration failures, missing/divergent/foreign targets,
+unreadable entries, both BTI landing pads, BTI kernel configuration, rearming,
+unchanged syscall frames, root policy changes, error ordering, concurrent rule
+updates and teardown while a callback's return tail is active. Deliberately
+unsafe variants are rejected. Kernel headers, incoming callback KCFI and module
+imports must still be verified by the pinned-DDK build.
+
+This route is limited to the four priority syscalls; no global syscall
+tracepoint, timer or scan is introduced. Probe breakpoint handling adds work on
+these calls. Hardware behavior and interaction with other runtime kernel hooks
+remain unverified until test4 is run on the phone. In particular, future code
+that redirects table entries after initialization can change coverage.
+
+HMA OSS uses Android framework package filtering in system_server rather than
+these kernel priority entry points, so this change adds no direct hook overlap.
+Keep RMGU out of HMA's filtered-caller scope so its PackageManager inventory can
+see inactive root managers. Hiding RMGU from other apps is compatible with that
+scope. No HMA templates/settings or inactive-manager names are changed here;
+phone coexistence still requires validation.
+
+References: https://docs.kernel.org/trace/kprobes.html and the source-pinned
+HMA review in the app repository, docs/susfs-hma-compatibility-20261031.md.
